Slots let one template receive content from another.

### Declaration and insert forms

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    card #= [:
        <h1>[$slot title = "Untitled"]</h1>
        <section>[$slot]</section>
    ]

    [card:
        [$in title:Welcome]
        Body content
    ]
]

The declaration and insert forms are:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    [$slot]                     -- default slot
    [$slot = "Fallback"]        -- default slot with a default
    [$slot name]                -- named slot
    [$slot name = "Fallback"]   -- named slot with a default
    [$slot 1]                   -- first positional slot
    [$slot 1 = "Fallback"]      -- positional slot with a default
    [$in name:body]             -- contribution to one named slot
]

Names and positions are bare source tokens, not String expressions or lexical
value bindings. `$slot "title"` and `$slot 0` are invalid. A position is a
positive whole number. A `$slot` declaration must be the only meaningful item in
its head and cannot open a body.

`$in` accepts a bare named target. It is not a String expression and it has no
positional form, so `$in "title"` is invalid. Each `$in` directive fills one
named slot and can appear once per head.

### Defaults

An occurrence can hold a compile-time `String` default, including a literal, a
constant binding, a cast or a structural resource path:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    fallback #= "Draft"

    card #= [:
        <h1>[$slot title = fallback]</h1>
        icon=[$slot image = @assets/icon.svg]
        alt=[$slot alt = "Icon"]
    ]
]

A default is an ordinary compile-time `String` value. It is not an arbitrary
expression evaluator and it is not a template wrapper value. A fully folded
template constant is an ordinary String and is accepted. A wrapper template
with unresolved slots is not. A nonconstant or non-String default is diagnosed
at the declaration.

Each slot occurrence owns its own default, even when another occurrence uses the
same key. A routed contribution, including an explicitly empty one, suppresses
that occurrence's default. An occurrence with no default and no routed
contribution renders empty. Repeated named occurrences consume the same routed
contributions but may fall back independently:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    card #= [:
        first=[$slot title = "First fallback"]
        second=[$slot title = "Second fallback"]
        body=[$slot = "Body fallback"]
    ]

    unset #= [card]
    chosen #= [card:[$in title:Chosen]]
]

Both `title` occurrences show `Chosen` in `chosen`. In `unset` they show their
separate fallbacks. Multiple contributions to one named slot concatenate in
source order. Contributions containing `A-` and `B-` fill both title occurrences
with `A-B-`. An explicitly empty insert still counts as routed.

An application with routed or loose content closes its own unfilled defaulted
occurrences: an enclosing template cannot fill them later. A head-only reference
with no fill stays an open wrapper. Slots authored in supplied content still
belong to their own enclosing composition.

### Positional slots

Positive integer slots receive loose head contributions. The number is a
declaration target, not an insert target:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    image = [:
        <img src="[$slot 1]" alt="[$slot]">
    ]

    [image, "logo.png": Site logo]
]

The first loose contribution fills slot `1`.

The remaining loose body content fills the default slot.

A positional declaration may also carry a default. Positional slots are never
filled by `$in`.

### Routing rules

- Loose contributions fill positional slots in ascending numeric order.
- Remaining loose contributions go to the default slot when one exists.
- Loose content that cannot be assigned is an error when no default slot exists.
- An occurrence with a default renders that default when nothing is routed to it.
- An unfilled occurrence without a default renders as an empty string.
- Repeated slots replay the same contribution.
- Named inserts target only their named slot and never fill positional or default slots.
- Multiple contributions to one named slot concatenate in source order.
- An unknown named insert target is diagnosed at the receiving template.
- One application may combine named, positional and default contributions.
- Formatting-only body whitespace around named inserts is not a loose contribution;
  an explicitly supplied empty or whitespace String remains a contribution.

### Builder root slots

A builder can register named destinations for direct top-level const inserts.
On an active entry root, a direct const `$in` routes to that builder destination
rather than to an ordinary template slot. Only a direct root insert routes. A
nested, stored or aliased insert remains ordinary template composition.

Builder slot values are builder-interpreted. Their fallbacks and output policies
belong to the selected builder, not to the language-level occurrence default
rule. See @../constants/#const-templates (Const templates) and
@../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).

Runtime top-level named builder inserts are not supported. Their final design
remains unsettled.

### Stored named inserts

Named inserts can be prepared in a binding and passed as loose contributions
through an immediate parent:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    card = [:
        <h1>[$slot title]</h1>
        [$slot]
    ]
    stored_title #= [$in title:Stored title]
    rendered #= [card: [stored_title] Body]
]

The stored helper is flattened into the immediate parent's slot-routing
stream. An insert with no matching immediate parent remains invalid.

Imported wrappers, re-exports and constant-record projections preserve their
slot defaults and routing.

### Runtime contributions

Runtime-capable templates support slot contributions from selected template
`if` branches and template loop bodies.

AST prepares the slot routing before HIR.

Runtime lowering appends the routed contributions through normal string
accumulators. Presence comes from routing, not from whether rendered content is
nonempty.

Unresolved slot or insert artefacts after routing are invalid template
structure.

### Deferred slot constraints

`$required` and `$unique` are deferred and not implemented. Loop-level
uniqueness is excluded, and const function syntax is not implemented. The
proposed forms are not accepted by the current parser:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    [$slot title, $required]   -- not implemented
    [$slot styles, $unique]    -- not implemented
]
